  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the coefficient of $(x-5)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ at $x=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-5)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=5$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(5)+{f}^{\u2032}(5)(x-5)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(5)}{2}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-5)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(5)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+4}=(x+4)^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x+4)^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(5)=-\\frac{1}{4}(5+4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {9}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{108}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-5)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(5)}{2}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{108})}{2}=-\\frac{1}{216}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
